  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes a liver–intestine–kidney signaling loop that links vitamin D with bile acid metabolism. In the liver, cholesterol is converted into bile acids through cholesterol 7α-monooxygenase, and bile acids are then released into the intestine, where they can be transformed into secondary bile acids such as LCA. The diagram suggests that bile acids and vitamin D act through nuclear receptors, especially VDR together with RXR, to regulate this pathway.\n\nA key idea is that the active vitamin D metabolite, 1,25(OH)2D, produced in connection with the kidney, signals both directly and indirectly. In enterocytes, bile acids/LCA and 1,25(OH)2D activate VDR/RXR, which then induces targets including MDRP, CYP3A4, and FGF15/19. These outputs are associated with bile acid reabsorption and LCA detoxification. FGF15/19 then appears to feed back to the liver through FGFR4.\n\nThe liver side also shows bile acid receptor signaling and inhibition of cholesterol 7α-monooxygenase, indicating negative feedback that limits further bile acid synthesis. Overall, the figure depicts coordinated endocrine and intestinal control of bile acid production, transport, and detoxification by vitamin D and bile acid receptor pathways.",
